so every type of mathematical object has  its own category  so there's a category of rings  whose objects include things like the  integers and the rational numbers  and the real numbers but then other  examples besides  so i'm using notation that'll be  familiar to mathematicians here  and any category in addition to its  objects has some notion of  transformation between those objects  which we draw  using arrows so these arrows represent  the inclusion for instance of the  rational numbers into the real numbers  the fundamental theorem of category  theory says that any mathematical object  no matter how complicated can be  understood can be characterized uniquely  by its relationship to the other objects  in its category  so for example in the category of rings  you might think of the integers as being  the numbers 0 1 2  negative 1 negative 2 together with  operations of addition and  multiplication but they're also uniquely  determined by the property that in the  category of the rings  there's a unique arrow from the integers  to any other ring  so you can think of a category as  providing a linguistic template for a  mathematical theory with nouns and verbs  but as the object's mathematicians study  get more complicated you need a more  robust linguistic template with  adjectives and adverbs and so on  and that's the idea of an infinity  category  so for example there's a category of  infinity rings  and like an ordinary category this one  has  objects and arrows so the examples have  some different names  but in addition to the objects and the  arrows which we think of as being zero  and one-dimensional  we have two-dimensional arrows arrows  between arrows that i draw  in this way and i think of as inhabiting  a two-dimensional space  and then there are also arrows between  arrows between arrows which we think of  as inhabiting the three-dimensional  space and arrows between arrows between  arrows between arrows and so on  all the way up this gets a little bit  harder to draw on paper  so mathematicians would like to use  infinite dimensional categories use  infinity categories to help systemize  their work in the way that  mathematicians are using ordinary  categories today  but the challenge with this is that it's  very difficult to write down the  definition of an infinity category  and in fact there are many competing  definitions which means that when  researchers  are proving theorems about infinity  categories they have to first pick a  specific definition  you can think of that as choosing  coordinates and then prove their  theorems in reference to that definition  so this provides a translation problem  you might ask if a theorem was proven  using one definition of infinity  categories  can be applied to an example of an  infinity category constructed in a  different definition  and even if that is possible  how exactly would that translation work  so this is the problem that i've been  working to solve with a collaborator  dominic verity from macquarie university  in sydney  and our approach is to apply the  philosophy of category theory to  category theory itself  so any type of mathematical object lives  in a category  these infinity categories are very  complicated objects themselves but if we  zoom out  we can think of them as living in their  own category  the category of infinity categories  and the category of infinity categories  has  some particular feature this universe in  which infinity categories live as  objects  we give it a name we call it an infinity  cosmos  so more precisely each model of infinity  categories  defines an infinity cosmos so if we  prove theorems about infinity cosmoy in  general then they specialize to give us  the theorems about  infinity categories proven in absolutely  every model and this is a way to  come up with a strategy for proving  theorems about infinity categories that  apply  universally
 

